---
// guides/what-is-a-top-level-domain.astro
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="What Is a Top-Level Domain? — TLD Directory"
  description="Learn what a top-level domain (TLD) is, how it fits into the DNS hierarchy, and the main categories of TLDs."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-8">
    <Breadcrumb items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
      { label: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
      { label: 'What Is a TLD?' }
    ]} />

    <article class="prose prose-ink max-w-none">
      <header class="mb-8">
        <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-ink-900">What Is a Top-Level Domain?</h1>
        <p class="mt-3 text-ink-600 text-lg">A clear explanation of TLDs, the DNS hierarchy, and the main categories.</p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>The short answer</h2>
        <p>A <strong>top-level domain (TLD)</strong> is the last segment of a domain name — the part after the final dot. In <code>example.com</code>, the TLD is <code>.com</code>. In <code>example.co.uk</code>, the TLD is <code>.uk</code> (with <code>.co</code> being a second-level domain).</p>
        <p>TLDs sit at the top of the Domain Name System (DNS) hierarchy. They are the first stop when a resolver looks up a domain name.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The DNS hierarchy</h2>
        <p>The DNS is a distributed, hierarchical naming system. From top to bottom:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Root zone</strong> — represented by an empty label (the trailing dot in <code>example.com.</code>). Managed by IANA/ICANN.</li>
          <li><strong>Top-level domains (TLDs)</strong> — <code>.com</code>, <code>.org</code>, <code>.uk</code>, <code>.中国</code>, etc.</li>
          <li><strong>Second-level domains</strong> — <code>example</code> in <code>example.com</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Subdomains</strong> — <code>www</code>, <code>mail</code>, <code>blog</code>, etc.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>When you type a domain in a browser, a recursive resolver starts at the root, asks for the TLD's authoritative name servers, then asks those for the second-level domain, and so on.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Main TLD categories</h2>
        <p>
          IANA's Root Zone Database index page was redesigned in 2026 and now publishes only three
          type values. Earlier pages used six. Both are described below so the older terms you may
          still find in blog posts still make sense.
        </p>
        <dl>
          <dt><strong>Generic TLDs (gTLDs)</strong></dt>
          <dd>
            Open to global registration. Examples: <code>.com</code>, <code>.org</code>, <code>.net</code>,
            <code>.dev</code>, <code>.app</code>, <code>.biz</code>. In IANA's current taxonomy
            <code>.gov</code>, <code>.edu</code>, <code>.museum</code> and <code>.aero</code> are
            also listed here, even though they were historically called <em>sponsored</em>.
          </dd>

          <dt><strong>Country-code TLDs (ccTLDs)</strong></dt>
          <dd>
            Two-letter codes from ISO 3166-1. Examples: <code>.uk</code>, <code>.de</code>, <code>.jp</code>,
            <code>.ai</code>, <code>.io</code>. Each is managed by a national registry with its own rules.
          </dd>

          <dt><strong>Special-use TLDs</strong></dt>
          <dd>
            IANA labels <code>.arpa</code> as "Special Use" — it is delegated but reserved for technical
            infrastructure. Eight further names (<code>.test</code>, <code>.example</code>,
            <code>.invalid</code>, <code>.localhost</code>, <code>.local</code>, <code>.internal</code>,
            <code>.alt</code>, <code>.onion</code>) share the label but are <strong>not delegated</strong>
            and never appear in the public DNS.
          </dd>

          <dt><strong>Internationalized TLDs (IDN TLDs)</strong></dt>
          <dd>
            A cross-cutting view, not an IANA type: TLDs stored as punycode and displayed in a native
            script (<code>.中国</code> → <code>.xn--fiqs8s</code>). Both generic and country-code IDNs exist.
          </dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="text-sm text-ink-600">
          Older IANA pages separated two further groups: <em>Sponsored TLDs</em> (.gov, .edu, .museum,
          .aero, .post) and <em>Generic Restricted TLDs</em> (.biz, .name, .pro). IANA no longer publishes
          those labels, so this site does not either — the eligibility rules they described are still set
          by each registry and are linked from the individual TLD pages.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Who runs the TLD system?</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>IANA (Internet Assigned Numbers Authority)</strong> — maintains the authoritative Root Zone Database and coordinates the root zone.</li>
          <li><strong>ICANN (Internet Corporation for Assigned Names and Numbers)</strong> — policy development and oversight of the DNS root.</li>
          <li><strong>Registry operators</strong> — run the authoritative name servers and registration database for a specific TLD (e.g. VeriSign for <code>.com</code>).</li>
          <li><strong>Registrars</strong> — accredited retailers that sell domain registrations to end users.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How many TLDs exist?</h2>
        <p>As of October 2026, there are <strong>over 1,400 delegated TLDs</strong> in the IANA root zone. The exact number grows as new gTLDs are delegated and ccTLDs are added or retired.</p>
        <p>You can browse the current list on our <a href="/tlds/">TLD directory</a> or download the authoritative list from <a href="https://data.iana.org/TLD/tlds-alpha-by-domain.txt" target="_blank" rel="noopener">IANA</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>A TLD is the highest level in the DNS hierarchy after the root.</li>
          <li>Every TLD has a registry operator responsible for its technical operation.</li>
          <li>IANA publishes three type values (Generic, Country Code, Special Use); the older "sponsored" and "generic-restricted" labels are no longer published.</li>
          <li>The IANA Root Zone Database is the single authoritative source for TLD data.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <footer class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-ink-200">
        <nav class="flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related guides">
          <a href="/guides/registry-vs-registrar/" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 font-medium">Next: Registry vs Registrar →</a>
          <a href="/guides/generic-vs-country-code-tlds/" class="text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 font-medium">Generic vs Country-Code TLDs →</a>
        </nav>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>